// QA joint scan×enrich×store identity-adoption e2e (tombstone-lifecycle task
// 2.4, Tier 1). The FULL production path — real cmd/mellifera subprocess + the
// six plugin subprocesses + the real enrich queue + the real SQLite ledger,
// over a real hermetic serial fixture library (qa_serial_e2e harness patterns,
// the qa_rescan_preservation stub idiom) — proves the tombstone adoption
// contract end to end (the JoJo acceptance at code level):
//
//   - tombstone + carry: renaming a serial directory tombstones its items via
//     the item sweep's stat miss (run.go sweepItems, the confirmed-clean-drain
//     branch) and the rescan creates identity-matched successor items; the
//     stranded playback_progress position and the watched_items ledger row a
//     viewer left on the vanished file land on the successor ids — the dead
//     rows hold NOTHING afterward (the moves' DELETE half, read RAW off the
//     server's SQLite — GetProgress/ListProgress hide deleted rows, so the
//     wire can never prove emptiness),
//   - the conflicting live row wins its slot: a viewer whose rows already sit
//     on the live successor keeps them byte-identical while their stranded
//     dead-id stock is deleted, never moved (the 2.1 NOT EXISTS slot guards,
//     proven through the real chain),
//   - the other viewer's stranded row still carries in the same wave.
//
// IDENTITY: the served identity is the _view.title.value floor plus the
// (season, episode) facts — so the rename keeps the PARSER title byte-stable
// the only way real disk can: the directory gains a bracketed release year
// ("Anchor Point S01" -> "Anchor Point S01 (2024)"); the releasename grammar
// strips the year tag, the derived search title is unchanged, and the stub
// upstream serves the SAME candidate across the rename. The episode rows'
// title floor is the ACTUAL post-scan state: episode documents never match at
// Match, a fresh scan's shikimori run is the single-fetch search shape (no
// extras, no episode titles, no route), so no episode row — old or successor —
// carries a _view.title; both floors read "" and the exact-equality compare
// passes on the shared empty floor, the (season, episode) legs deciding. That
// empty-floor legs-only identity IS the system's actual one-wave convergence
// (adopt.go's header claim), and this joint pins it.
//
// THE CONFLICT LEG re-fires adoption deterministically through the real
// operator surface: POST /libraries/{id}/refresh (plugin serial-parser, the
// successor row ids) strips + re-runs the parser namespace, re-settles the
// rows, and the settle hook re-fires adoption — this time with B's live rows
// already on the successor (the NOT EXISTS guards must refuse the stranded
// dead-id stock and delete it) and A's fresh stranded stock on E03-old (the
// other viewer's row must still carry).
//
// HERMETIC: the shikimori upstream is pinned to a local httptest stub through
// MELLIFERA_SHIKIMORI_UPSTREAM (no test byte reaches shikimori.io); library
// roots anchor under t.TempDir(); fixture identity constants are independent
// of every other qa fixture (no-coincidence discipline); every helper is
// qaTomb-prefixed to stay collision-free in package itest. Files are 120 s
// lavfi mkv (the qaAcctMedia generator) so every staged position sits BELOW
// the completion detector's floor (0.9 × 120 = 108) — no accidental
// completions.
//
// CONTRACT MISMATCH (implementation defect, flagged to the session — see the
// phase-2 comment for the evidence chain): the dead rows' (item, shikimori)
// pair is perpetually pending (skips write no ledger, pendingSources has no
// attemptedNoWork check), so every derive pass re-dispatches a dead row's
// workItem; its skip-settle fires the unguarded adoption hook
// (queue.go:750 — no live-status guard), and AdoptStranded then treats the
// dead row as its own live item: the self-move's NOT EXISTS sees the source
// row itself, inserts nothing, and the unconditional DELETE destroys the
// dead id's whole stock (store/adopt.go) — silently, as "carried nothing".
// Derivation walks ORDER BY id over RANDOM hex ids, so destroyer-vs-carrier
// ordering is random per run: the carry this task exists to prove races its
// own destruction. The assertions below state the CONTRACT (the acceptance's
// expected end state); a red here is the defect's receipt, not a test bug.
//
// THE NO-CROSS-CARRY WAVE (task 2.7, post-red): the fixture gains a SECOND
// series — "Beacon Line S01" -> "Beacon Line S01 (2019)", four episodes, E04
// REMOVED at the rename so its dead row has NO successor. The amended chain
// is proved end to end through the real scan→queue→adopt path: the series
// bridge (2.5) routes B's dead stock onto B's own successors and REFUSES
// every cross-series pair (equal (season, episode) legs, "" item floors on
// both sides — the production library-3 collision — discriminated ONLY by the
// parser-composed series titles, which the bracketed-tag rename keeps
// byte-stable). The queue's documented dynamics shape the waves: adoption
// re-fires at every live settle (live rows with an absent namespace
// re-dispatch every derive pass — the terminal-stamp gap 2.6 rules out of
// scope), so a rename's first-wave adoption fire floors "" (the new series
// row's parser title lands on its own settle, which the drain order puts
// after the item settles) and the carry CONVERGES on the next pass — the
// fixture runs that convergence pass explicitly before 2.4's wave-1 asserts,
// whose bytes stay intact; B's seeded stock is consumed by the FIRST
// identity-matched pass (2.4's own wave-2 refresh, which thereby doubles as a
// cross-carry detector with B's stock in play); and only a dead row with NO
// successor (B-E04-old) retains its stock across EVERY queue pass — retention
// is the no-match law, consumption the matched law.
